<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- The shared Avalonia toolkit: theme, controls, window scaffolding and a
       fluent layout surface, so EmuSen.Mistress, EmuSen.Hotaru and the future
       launcher stop rebuilding chrome from raw primitives. See
       EmuSen_LunaP.md for what exists and EmuSen_LunaP_Gameplan.md for what
       is planned; named for Luna-P, Chibiusa's gadget ball (naming scheme
       §11 - distinct from Luna, the reserved DS core codename).

       THE LAYERING RULE, and it is load-bearing: this project may reference
       AVALONIA AND NOTHING ELSE. Not EmuSen (the core), not EmuSen.DianaOS,
       not EmuSen.Endymion, and no longer EmuSen.Galaxia or EmuSen.Cauldron
       either. A test asserts it - see EmuSen_LunaP.md §19.3.

       The rule used to admit Galaxia and Cauldron, on the argument that both
       are core-free leaves and referencing them costs a consumer one small
       assembly rather than an emulator. That argument was sound and it is not
       why the rule changed. It changed because this toolkit is going to its
       own repository, where a reference to anything called EmuSen is not a
       layering question but a dependency somebody outside this project cannot
       resolve - §19.

       What went, and where: Windowing/WindowPlacementStore.cs and
       Theme/LunaTheme.cs read through Settings/ISettingsStore instead of
       Galaxia's ConfigFile, and a host points that at whatever it keeps its
       settings in (§19.1). Input/DefaultPadKeyMap.cs moved to EmuSen.Endymion,
       which already owns the mapping of physical input onto PadButton.
       Dashboards/CoretopWindow.cs moved to EmuSen.Serenity, which already
       holds the core-agnostic Avalonia layer on the video side. Neither was
       ever general: one names a console gamepad and the other reads emulator
       telemetry, and a toolkit that knows about either is not a toolkit.

       Every CONTROL here takes plain data or a delegate, which is the same
       rule stated at the level of a class rather than an assembly. See
       EmuSen_LunaP.md §1. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>true</AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Packed so EmuSen.Pegasus can consume the toolkit from its own repository
       rather than a ProjectReference across a repo boundary. This does not
       relax the layering rule above; it is what enforces it, because a package
       cannot reach back up into a core. See EmuSen_LunaP.md §17. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>EmuSen.LunaP</PackageId>
    <Version>0.1.0</Version>
    <Authors>Tristan Pelland</Authors>
    <Description>Shared Avalonia toolkit for EmuSen: theme, controls, window scaffolding and a fluent layout surface.</Description>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>GPL-3.0-or-later</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/RedQuE3n/EmuSen-Project</RepositoryUrl>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Versions pinned to match EmuSen.Mistress exactly, the convention
         EmuSen.Serenity's csproj already follows. Desktop/Fonts.Inter/X11 are
         here for LunaApp.Configure, which owns the UsePlatformDetect/
         WithInterFont/LogToTrace/UseX11 sequence both frontends' Program.cs
         used to spell out separately - see EmuSen_LunaP.md §3. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="12.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Desktop" Version="12.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Markup.Xaml.Loader" Version="12.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" Version="12.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Fonts.Inter" Version="12.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.X11" Version="12.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
